" (https://www.cacb.uscourts.gov/sites/cacb/files/documents/general-orders/GO%2024-01.pdf)\nciteturn1020view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 2; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn829view2\",\"id\":4}); Total lines: 95\nL0@P0: -1-\nL1@P0: 1\nL2@P0: 2\nL3@P0: 3\nL4@P0: 4\nL5@P0: 5\nL6@P0: 6\nL7@P0: 7\nL8@P0: 8\nL9@P0: 9\nL10@P0: 10\nL11@P0: 11\nL12@P0: 12\nL13@P0: 13\nL14@P0: 14\nL15@P0: 15\nL16@P0: 16\nL17@P0: 17\nL18@P0: 18\nL19@P0: 19\nL20@P0: 20\nL21@P0: 21\nL22@P0: 22\nL23@P0: 23\nL24@P0: 24\nL25@P0: 25\nL26@P0: 26\nL27@P0: 27\nL28@P0: 28\nL29@P0: UNITED STATES BANKRUPTCY COURT\nL30@P0: CENTRAL DISTRICT OF CALIFORNIA\nL31@P0: In re: PROCEDURES FOR LOAN\nL32@P0: MODIFICATION MANAGEMENT\nL33@P0: PROGRAM\nL34@P0: GENERAL ORDER 24-01\nL35@P0: The United States Congress created procedures to follow when parties seek to\nL36@P0: mitigate losses when a real estate loan is in distress, namely 12 CFR 1024 (Regulation\nL37@P0: X, Loss Mitigation Procedures). While in bankruptcy court, it can be difficult for the\nL38@P0: parties to invoke and comply with these statutory requirements in conjunction with\nL39@P0: relevant provisions of the Bankruptcy Code (“Code”) and Federal Rules of Bankruptcy\nL40@P0: Procedure (“FRBP”).\nL41@P0: In 2017, U.S. Bankruptcy Court, Central District of California (USBC-CD) created\nL42@P0: a pilot program to meet this challenge. In 2023, the Board of Judges voted to make this\nL43@P0: program permanent. The title of this program is the Loan Modification Management\nL44@P0: Program (“LMM”) as implemented in the Central District of California within regulations\nL45@P0: referred to above, and within our Local Bankruptcy Rules (“LBR”).\nL46@P0-1: JAN 25, 2024-2-\nL47@P1: 1\nL48@P1: 2\nL49@P1: 3\nL50@P1: 4\nL51@P1: 5\nL52@P1: 6\nL53@P1: 7\nL54@P1: 8\nL55@P1: 9\nL56@P1: 10\nL57@P1: 11\nL58@P1: 12\nL59@P1: 13\nL60@P1: 14\nL61@P1: 15\nL62@P1: 16\nL63@P1: 17\nL64@P1: 18\nL65@P1: 19\nL66@P1: 20\nL67@P1: 21\nL68@P1: 22\nL69@P1: 23\nL70@P1: 24\nL71@P1: 25\nL72@P1: 26\nL73@P1: 27\nL74@P1: 28\nL75@P1: The LMM is managed by a vendor approved by the USBC-CD and is a forum for\nL76@P1: debtors and creditors to reach a consensual resolution – or loss mitigation -- when a\nL77@P1: debtor’s Eligible Property is at risk of foreclosure related to a creditor’s Eligible Loan.\nL78@P1: Loss mitigation means the full range of solutions that may prevent either the loss of a\nL79@P1: Debtor’s Eligible Property to foreclosure, increased costs to the Creditor, or both,\nL80@P1: including but not limited to loan modification, loan refinance, forbearance, short sale, or\nL81@P1: surrender of the Eligible Property in full satisfaction of obligations arising under an\nL82@P1: Eligible Loan. The goal of the LMM is to facilitate communication and exchange of\nL83@P1: information in a confidential setting and encourage the parties to finalize a feasible and\nL84@P1: beneficial agreement under Court supervision.\nL85@P1: IT IS ORDERED: Participants in the LMM must comply with LMM Procedures\nL86@P1: and LMM Forms that are posted in section 3-10 of The Central Guide and at related\nL87@P1: links on the court’s website. Special Tab: LOAN MODIFICATION MANAGEMENT\nL88@P1: PROGRAM | Central District of California | United States Bankruptcy Court\nL89@P1: (uscourts.gov)\nL90@P1: DATED: January 25, 2024\nL91@P1: \nL92@P1:  THEODOR C. ALBERT\nL93@P1:  Chief Judge\nL94@P1:  United States Bankruptcy Court\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.cacb.uscourts.gov/sites/cacb/files/documents/general-orders/GO%2022-03.pdf)\nciteturn1020view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last year; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 2; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn829view2\",\"id\":8}); Total lines: 85\nL0@P0: -1-\nL1@P0: 1\nL2@P0: 2\nL3@P0: 3\nL4@P0: 4\nL5@P0: 5\nL6@P0: 6\nL7@P0: 7\nL8@P0: 8\nL9@P0: 9\nL10@P0: 10\nL11@P0: 11\nL12@P0: 12\nL13@P0: 13\nL14@P0: 14\nL15@P0: 15\nL16@P0: 16\nL17@P0: 17\nL18@P0: 18\nL19@P0: 19\nL20@P0: 20\nL21@P0: 21\nL22@P0: 22\nL23@P0: 23\nL24@P0: 24\nL25@P0: 25\nL26@P0: 26\nL27@P0: 27\nL28@P0: 28\nL29@P0: UNITED STATES BANKRUPTCY COURT\nL30@P0: CENTRAL DISTRICT OF CALIFORNIA\nL31@P0: CORONAVIRUS PUBLIC EMERGENCY –\nL32@P0: ORDER RESCINDING POLICY\nL33@P0: REGARDING COVID-19 VACCINATION\nL34@P0: AND TESTING\nL35@P0: GENERAL ORDER 22-03\nL36@P0: (Supersedes General Order 21-06)\nL37@P0: Upon consideration of the order of United States District Court General Order\nL38@P0: 22-05,\nL39@P0: IT IS HEREBY ORDERED as follows:\nL40@P0: The Court’s previously issued General Order 21-06, which included the “Policy\nL41@P0: Regarding COVID-19 Vaccination and Testing” followed by the amended version\nL42@P0: of the Policy (viewable at https://www.cacb.uscourts.gov/sites/cacb/files/documents/\nL43@P0: Amended%20Vac.Policy.AttestForm.Faq.pdf) as described in Public Notice 22-006, is\nL44@P0: rescinded; and\nL45@P0: IT IS FURTHER ORDERED that, although the Court rescinds the “Amended\nL46@P0: Policy Regarding COVID-19 Vaccination and Testing” through this Order, all\nL47@P0: employees, volunteers, and onsite judiciary contractors are still strongly encouraged to\nL48@P0-1: DEC 01 2022-2-\nL49@P1: 1\nL50@P1: 2\nL51@P1: 3\nL52@P1: 4\nL53@P1: 5\nL54@P1: 6\nL55@P1: 7\nL56@P1: 8\nL57@P1: 9\nL58@P1: 10\nL59@P1: 11\nL60@P1: 12\nL61@P1: 13\nL62@P1: 14\nL63@P1: 15\nL64@P1: 16\nL65@P1: 17\nL66@P1: 18\nL67@P1: 19\nL68@P1: 20\nL69@P1: 21\nL70@P1: 22\nL71@P1: 23\nL72@P1: 24\nL73@P1: 25\nL74@P1: 26\nL75@P1: 27\nL76@P1: 28\nL77@P1: follow the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention’s guidance regarding COVID-19\nL78@P1: prevention measures, including staying up to date with their COVID-19 vaccines.\nL79@P1: IT IS SO ORDERED.\nL80@P1: DATED: December 1, 2022\nL81@P1: \nL82@P1:  THEODOR C. ALBERT\nL83@P1:  Chief Judge\nL84@P1:  United States Bankruptcy Court\n-----------------------------------------------------